      "blocks": [
        {
          "type": "header",
          "bbox": [
            0.138,
            0.047,
            0.418,
            0.065
          ],
          "content": "Ganita Prakash | Grade 8 | Part-II"
        },
        {
          "type": "paragraph_title",
          "bbox": [
            0.152,
            0.09,
            0.784,
            0.113
          ],
          "level": 2,
          "content": "Finding the Area Using Two Copies of the Trapezium"
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.151,
            0.116,
            0.846,
            0.173
          ],
          "content": "There is another interesting way of finding the area of a trapezium. Consider two copies of the given trapezium in which AB‖CD. Rotate the second copy as shown."
        },
        {
          "type": "image",
          "bbox": [
            0.267,
            0.176,
            0.48,
            0.27
          ],
          "content": "",
          "captions": [],
          "footnotes": [],
          "image_source": "images/page_20_image_3.jpg"
        },
        {
          "type": "image",
          "bbox": [
            0.509,
            0.173,
            0.721,
            0.27
          ],
          "content": "",
          "captions": [],
          "footnotes": [],
          "image_source": "images/page_20_image_4.jpg"
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.114,
            0.275,
            0.847,
            0.338
          ],
          "content": "？ What figure will we get when the two trapeziums are joined along BC? The possibilities are either a 6-sided figure or a 4-sided figure (quadrilateral)."
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.152,
            0.348,
            0.287,
            0.368
          ],
          "content": "**Possibilities**"
        },
        {
          "type": "image",
          "bbox": [
            0.131,
            0.367,
            0.469,
            0.534
          ],
          "content": "",
          "captions": [],
          "footnotes": [],
          "image_source": "images/page_20_image_7.jpg"
        },
        {
          "type": "image",
          "bbox": [
            0.496,
            0.385,
            0.86,
            0.484
          ],
          "content": "",
          "captions": [],
          "footnotes": [
            {
              "bbox": [
                0.654,
                0.487,
                0.758,
                0.503
              ],
              "content": "Quadrilateral"
            },
            {
              "bbox": [
                0.19,
                0.533,
                0.296,
                0.549
              ],
              "content": "6-sided figure"
            }
          ],
          "image_source": "images/page_20_image_8.jpg"
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.151,
            0.556,
            0.846,
            0.631
          ],
          "content": "We can rule out the first possibility by looking at the sum of angles x and y. Since AB ‖ CD, we have $x + y = 180$ , since they are internal angles along the same side of the transversal (BC). So ABD́ and A ́CD are straight lines. Therefore, the resulting figure is a quadrilateral."
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.111,
            0.632,
            0.506,
            0.655
          ],
          "content": "What type of a quadrilateral is this?"
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.151,
            0.657,
            0.659,
            0.677
          ],
          "content": "Let us look at the other two angles of the trapezium."
        },
        {
          "type": "image",
          "bbox": [
            0.312,
            0.681,
            0.679,
            0.779
          ],
          "content": "",
          "captions": [],
          "footnotes": [],
          "image_source": "images/page_20_image_14.jpg"
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.152,
            0.787,
            0.846,
            0.844
          ],
          "content": "We have $u + v = 180$ . Therefore, AD‖D́ A ́, since the sum of the internal angles along the same side of the transversal A ́D is 180°. Since we already have AD́  ‖ A ́D, the quadrilateral AD́ A ́D is actually a parallelogram."
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.08,
            0.912,
            0.131,
            0.935
          ],
          "content": "168"
        },
        {
          "type": "image",
          "bbox": [
            0.216,
